To solve this problem, we will create a custom React Context called TooltipContext. This context will be responsible for managing the state of which tooltip is currently open.

The TooltipContext will provide the following features:

1. A function to open a tooltip.
2. A function to close all the tooltips.
3. A function to handle click events on tooltips.

By using the TooltipContext, we can manage the state of the tooltip globally and easily control which tooltip is currently open and which one should be closed. This makes it easier to create complex interactions between tooltips and other components in the app.

Let's implement it.

// Context.js

import React, { createContext, useContext, useState } from 'react';

const TooltipContext = createContext();

const TooltipProvider = ({ children }) => {
const [currentTooltip, setCurrentTooltip] = useState(null);

const openTooltip = (id) => {
setCurrentTooltip(id);
};

const closeTooltip = () => {
setCurrentTooltip(null);
};

const handleTooltipClick = (id) => {
if (currentTooltip === id) {
closeTooltip();
} else {
setCurrentTooltip(id);
}
};

return (
<TooltipContext.Provider value={{ openTooltip, closeTooltip, handleTooltipClick, currentTooltip }}>
{children}
</TooltipContext.Provider>
);
};

const useTooltip = () => useContext(TooltipContext);

export { TooltipProvider, useTooltip };

// Tooltip.js

import React, { useState, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';
import { useTooltip } from './Context';

const Tooltip = ({ content, children, id }) => {
const [isHovered, setIsHovered] = useState(false);
const [showTooltip, setShowTooltip] = useState(false);
const tooltipRef = useRef(null);
const { openTooltip, handleTooltipClick, currentTooltip } = useTooltip();

useEffect(() => {
if (isHovered || showTooltip) {
openTooltip(id);
} else if (currentTooltip === id) {
handleTooltipClick(id);
}
}, [isHovered, showTooltip, id]);

const handleClick = () => {
handleTooltipClick(id);
setShowTooltip(!showTooltip);
};

return (
<div className="relative">
<div onMouseEnter={() => setIsHovered(true)} onMouseLeave={() => setIsHovered(false)} onClick={handleClick}>
{children}
</div>
{currentTooltip === id && (
<div ref={tooltipRef} className="absolute top-0 right-0 bg-gray-200 p-2 rounded-md">
{content}
</div>
)}
</div>
);
};

export default Tooltip;

// App.js

import React from 'react';
import Tooltip from './Tooltip';
import { TooltipProvider } from './Context';

const App = () => {
return (
<TooltipProvider>
<Tooltip content="This is the first tooltip" id="1">
<button>Tooltip 1</button>
</Tooltip>
<Tooltip content="This is the second tooltip" id="2">
<button>Tooltip 2</button>
</Tooltip>
</TooltipProvider>
);
};

export default App;

In this example, the TooltipProvider is used to wrap the entire application, and the Tooltip components are then used within it. When a user hovers over a tooltip button, it opens the corresponding tooltip. If another tooltip is opened, it closes the previous one automatically.

The Tooltip component uses the useTooltip hook to access the TooltipContext and call the openTooltip and handleTooltipClick functions. It also uses a ref to access the DOM element of the tooltip.

Note: This is just a simple implementation and may need to be customized and improved depending on the specific requirements of your application.

